# Supabase project → Settings → API
SUPABASE_URL=https://YOUR_PROJECT.supabase.co
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=eyJ...
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=eyJ...   # server only, never ship to the browser

# OPTIONAL, and only for projects still on the legacy shared JWT secret.
#
# Access tokens are verified on this server instead of by a network call to
# Supabase Auth on every request (src/lib/jwt.js) — that round-trip is what
# breaks first when a hundred students share one campus Wi-Fi IP, because
# GoTrue rate-limits per IP.
#
# Modern projects sign tokens asymmetrically (ES256) and are verified against
# the project's published JWKS, which needs NO config at all — check yours at
#   https://YOUR_PROJECT.supabase.co/auth/v1/.well-known/jwks.json
# A non-empty `keys` array plus an ES256 token means you can leave this blank.
#
# Set it only if your project still issues HS256 tokens: Supabase dashboard →
# Settings → API → JWT Secret. Wrong value = ignored with a warning at boot
# (it's checked against the anon key), never a lockout. Unset = those tokens
# are verified by Supabase Auth exactly as they were before.
#
# Treat it like the service-role key: it can mint valid tokens. Never ship it
# to the browser.
# SUPABASE_JWT_SECRET=

PORT=3000

# Which deployment this is. Anything other than `production` marks the app as a
# TEST deployment: the three PWAs are served with a "TEST " name and a crimson
# theme colour so the installed icon can't be confused with the real one, the
# page title gains a TEST prefix, and each service worker's cache name gets the
# app's content hash appended so every deploy invalidates its own cache (no more
# hand-bumping CACHE in sw.js to see a change on your phone).
#
# LEAVE THIS UNSET IN PRODUCTION. Unset means production, so a misconfigured
# environment fails toward "treat me as live" rather than the other way round.
# The staging Heroku app sets APP_ENV=staging; so does local dev.
# APP_ENV=staging

# Public origin, used to build the punch-card QR links. Set it wherever the
# browser-visible origin isn't what the dyno sees (i.e. behind Cloudflare);
# otherwise the request's own host is correct.
# APP_ORIGIN=https://we-rewards.com

# Operator dashboard at /admin — comma-separated emails allowed to see analytics
# + error logs. Must match the Google account you sign in with. Leave blank to
# lock everyone out of /admin.
ADMIN_EMAILS=you@example.com

# Operator sign-in for the VENDOR TERMINAL at /terminal. Setting BOTH of these
# creates a real Supabase account at boot (and re-syncs its password on every
# boot, so this file is the source of truth) which can open ANY vendor's till
# from the store menu at the top left and work it exactly as that vendor:
# scanning, awarding, redeeming, items, deals and settings, with the staff PIN
# not asked for. Leave either blank and the account is never created and the
# feature does not exist.
#
# This is a SEPARATE grant from ADMIN_EMAILS above, on purpose. That list is
# Google-gated and read-mostly; this is one password with write access to every
# shop on the platform. Use a DEDICATED address that is not in ADMIN_EMAILS —
# reusing one gives that Google account an email+password identity too, so
# guessing this string would also open /admin. Boot warns if you do.
#
# The password must be at least 16 characters (72 max — bcrypt ignores the
# rest); anything shorter switches the feature off with a warning rather than
# accepting it. Sign-in goes to Supabase GoTrue, not to this app, so none of
# the express-rate-limit ceilings in server.js apply to guessing it. Generate
# one, don't invent one:  openssl rand -base64 24
#
# Rotating it = change the value here and restart. Note that the terminal hides
# its own "Login password" card for this account, since a change made there
# would be silently reverted by the next deploy.
#
# Needs migration-054 applied: without it the nightly abandoned-signup sweep
# deletes this account (it has no profile, no vendor link and no consent record,
# which is exactly what that job collects). Boot prints which state it is in —
# if it says OFF, read the line, it says why.
# TERMINAL_ADMIN_EMAIL=terminal-admin@example.com
# TERMINAL_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# Trusted-proxy hop count for correct client IPs / rate limiting. Default 1 (one
# PaaS proxy). Set to 2 behind e.g. Cloudflare→Render, or false with no proxy.
# TRUST_PROXY=1

# Web-push keys, shared by admin application/error alerts and student deal
# notifications. Generate once with: npx web-push generate-vapid-keys
# Leave blank to disable push silently (the rest of the app is unaffected —
# campaigns still queue and still show in every student's in-app Deals list).
VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:you@example.com

# ---- Receipt scanning: the AI reader ----
# Key from https://aistudio.google.com/apikey. With it set, an uploaded receipt
# goes to Google's Gemini API, which BOTH checks the photo is a genuine printed
# receipt (a screenshot or a photo of a screen is rejected — tesseract cannot
# tell the difference and will happily pay out on one) AND reads the vendor,
# total, and printed date/time straight off the image.
#
# Leave blank and receipt scanning still works exactly as before, via the
# bundled tesseract engine — it just loses the forgery check. Tesseract also
# stays as the automatic fallback whenever Google can't be reached: no key,
# quota exhausted, timeout, or outage. A forgery verdict is NEVER retried
# through tesseract; that would undo the check.
#
# Sending student receipt photos to Google is disclosed in the Privacy Policy
# (§2.10 and §4). If you revise those documents, bump TERMS_VERSION in
# src/lib/terms.js to re-prompt students for consent.
#
# Verify your key and model before shipping:  npm run check:gemini
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# Optional. Defaults shown. GEMINI_MODEL exists so a deprecated or misspelled
# model id is an .env edit rather than a deploy — any Gemini model that accepts
# images works. Flash-Lite is the cheapest multimodal tier and has a free quota;
# 3.5 costs $0.30/$2.50 per 1M input/output tokens on the paid tier, which is
# roughly $0.002 per receipt. GEMINI_TIMEOUT_MS is deliberately tight: the
# request still has to run tesseract afterwards if this call gives up, and the
# whole thing must finish inside Heroku's 30s H12 window.
# GEMINI_MODEL=gemini-3.5-flash-lite
# GEMINI_TIMEOUT_MS=9000

# ---- Vendor deals: the anti-storm fences (migration-032) ----
# Defaults are the pilot's and are documented in the Privacy Policy (§7.4), so
# loosening the caps means revising that document and bumping TERMS_VERSION.
# Every value below is optional; the shipped defaults are shown.
#
# CAMPAIGN_COOLDOWN_MINUTES=240      # hard floor between two notifications to one student
# CAMPAIGN_DAILY_CAP=2               # ...per rolling day
# CAMPAIGN_WEEKLY_CAP=5              # ...per rolling week
# CAMPAIGN_COALESCE_MINUTES=5        # hold before release; 0 disables bundling entirely
# CAMPAIGN_VENDOR_COOLDOWN_HOURS=20  # one vendor to one student
# CAMPAIGN_VENDOR_WEEKLY_SENDS=2     # sends a vendor gets per rolling week
# CAMPAIGN_BUNDLE_MAX=4              # vendors named in one digest
# CAMPAIGN_QUIET_START=22            # campus-local quiet hours [start, end)
# CAMPAIGN_QUIET_END=9               # set both to the same value to disable
# CAMPAIGN_TIMEZONE=America/New_York # falls back to PUNCH_TIMEZONE
# CAMPAIGN_DURATION_HOURS=48         # default life of a deal
# CAMPAIGN_TICK_SECONDS=30           # delivery worker interval
# CAMPAIGN_BATCH_USERS=40            # students settled per tick

# ---- Incentives: referral settlement (migration-039) ----
# How often the sweep looks for referrals whose friend has now earned points,
# and pays the referrer. Deliberately a background poll rather than a hook on
# the award path: nothing a cashier does may fail because of a referral bug.
# Lowering it only makes a bonus arrive sooner; the sweep is idempotent.
#
# REFERRAL_SWEEP_SECONDS=45          # floor is 10; the payout is never on a request path

# ---- Email (Resend) ----
# The stack's only mail transport. Optional in the same way web push is: with
# RESEND_API_KEY unset the whole thing degrades to a silent no-op and everything
# else runs unchanged. What stops happening, quietly, is worth knowing:
#
#   • a /join applicant gets no confirmation that their application landed;
#   • an accepted vendor is never told they can sign in (and, when their email
#     already had an account, never told WHICH password works);
#   • "Email me a code" on the terminal's Forgot password screen mints nothing
#     at all, so a locked-out vendor has to phone for an operator-read code;
#   • deal emails never send — and email is the ONLY channel that reaches a
#     student whose browser has no web push, which on iOS is everyone who did
#     not install the PWA.
#
# Verify before shipping:  npm run check:resend
#                          npm run check:resend -- you@example.com   (send samples)
#
# The key comes from https://resend.com/api-keys. A sending-only key is enough.
RESEND_API_KEY=

# Must be an address on a domain you have VERIFIED in Resend (SPF + DKIM). The
# display name is not decoration: a bare address in the From line is one of the
# cheapest spam signals there is. resend.dev addresses only reach the Resend
# account owner, so they look fine in testing and fail on the first real vendor.
EMAIL_FROM=WeRewards <hello@we-rewards.com>

# Optional. Where a vendor's reply lands. Worth setting: an address that bounces
# replies teaches people the sender is a robot they cannot reach, which is
# exactly when they press "Report spam" instead.
# EMAIL_REPLY_TO=hello@we-rewards.com

# Optional. Bounce and spam-complaint handling — the email counterpart of the
# 404/410 endpoint prune in src/lib/push.js. Without it, a dead address is
# mailed forever and the resulting bounce rate decays the domain's reputation
# until the PASSWORD RESETS stop arriving too.
#
# In Resend: Webhooks -> add https://YOUR_DOMAIN/api/webhooks/resend, subscribe
# to email.bounced and email.complained, then paste the signing secret here.
# Every request is Svix-signed; with this unset the endpoint refuses everything
# rather than trusting the caller, because a forged event can suppress an
# address and lock a vendor out of recovery.
# RESEND_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_...

# Optional. HMAC key for the one-click unsubscribe links in deal emails.
# Defaults to SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY, which is already required and already
# secret — so a deployment can never end up with an EMPTY signing key, which is
# the failure that would make every unsubscribe link forgeable. Set this only if
# you want to rotate unsubscribe links independently; rotating it invalidates
# every link already sitting in an inbox.
# EMAIL_UNSUB_SECRET=

# Optional. How long to wait on the Resend API. Deliberately tight: nothing is
# waiting on the answer (every caller has either already responded or is a
# background worker), so a slow mail API should cost one queued email rather
# than a held request.
# EMAIL_TIMEOUT_MS=10000
#
# NOTE: APP_ORIGIN (above) stops being optional once email is on. Mail is
# composed by a background worker with no request to infer an origin from, so
# every link in every email is built from it.

# ---- Product analytics (PostHog) ----
# Optional, and a MIRROR — never the system of record. Every event still goes to
# the client_events table (migration-024) exactly as before; when a key is set,
# src/lib/events.js also forwards it to PostHog. With POSTHOG_API_KEY unset the
# module is inert: no queue, no timer, no outbound request.
#
# This is a SERVER-SIDE integration. No posthog-js runs in the browser, so the
# CSP stays script-src 'self', the es2017/safari12 bundle floor is untouched, and
# no service-worker cache needs bumping. What that costs, concretely:
#
#   • no autocapture, no session replay, no feature flags — those need the
#     browser SDK, which is a public/ change this deliberately does not make;
#   • events fired before sign-in (pwa_launched, most of the install funnel)
#     have no per-device id, so they are sent WITHOUT a person profile. They are
#     queryable as counts and breakdowns, but PostHog cannot stitch them into a
#     true funnel. Signed-in events are attributed to the Supabase user id and
#     behave normally.
#
# Verify before trusting it:  npm run check:posthog
#
# The project key is the "Project API Key" in PostHog under Settings → Project.
# It starts with phc_ and is safe to expose (it is write-only ingestion) — but
# there is no reason to ship it to the browser here, so it stays server-side.
POSTHOG_API_KEY=

# Region matters: a US key posted to the EU host is rejected, and vice versa.
# US cloud (the posthog.com signup default):  https://us.i.posthog.com
# EU cloud:                                   https://eu.i.posthog.com
# Self-hosted:                                your own origin
# Defaults to US cloud when unset.
# POSTHOG_HOST=https://us.i.posthog.com
